# What a HADES ingest of this workspace does not read.
#
# **A workspace declares its own exclusions.** The ingest is configured on the
# server and the corpus lives here, so a rule kept only on the server is a
# rule the tree cannot be checked against. Per issue #565.
#
# Syntax follows .gitignore: one pattern per line, `#` comments, `/` anchors
# to this file's directory, a trailing `/` matches directories only.
#
# **Only what this workspace adds.** The server already skips `__pycache__`,
# `node_modules`, `target` and `venv` by default and hidden directories with
# them, so listing those here is a copy that drifts when the server's defaults
# move rather than a rule this tree is stating. Both entries below are
# load-bearing.

# **Frozen copies of live code.** An ingest walking one puts the same symbols
# in twice under two paths, so symbol resolution has two answers and no basis
# for choosing, and retrieval returns the stale text as readily as the live.
#
# `WeaverTools-Working-Process` section 1 carries the rule: git is the archive
# and no archive directory stands in this tree at all. The two that did were
# deleted at PR #563 and `census.py`'s `archive_directories` reading holds it
# at zero. **This is the second half of that rule and not a substitute for
# it** - it covers the window between a directory appearing and someone acting
# on the finding.
#
# **A family of spellings**, because a rule naming today's instance is the same
# mistake as deleting today's instance. The reading matches the same set.
# gitignore syntax has no case-insensitivity flag and the census reading uses
# `re.I`, so the case classes are written out. Without them `ARCHIVE/` is
# ingested while the census flags it - the two halves of one rule disagreeing,
# which CodeRabbit found on PR #566.
#
# **Eight patterns, and the reading is bounded so eight is enough.** Two
# prefixes by two plural forms by two suffix forms. The reading allowed any
# number of leading underscores and gitignore cannot say that, which left the
# halves 93 names apart on a second reading. `test_census.py` generates the
# whole cross-product and fails on any disagreement, rather than checking a
# list somebody wrote out - which is how the first eight passed.
**/[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee]/
**/[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][SsDd]/
**/[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][-_]*/
**/[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][SsDd][-_]*/
**/_[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee]/
**/_[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][SsDd]/
**/_[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][-_]*/
**/_[Aa][Rr][Cc][Hh][Ii][Vv][Ee][SsDd][-_]*/

# **Experiments are a separate corpus and left this tree** on 2026-09-13, per
# Working Process section 5 and issue #564. Kept after the departure for the
# same reason the archive patterns are kept after the deletion: the rule is
# about the class and not about today's directory.
experiments/

# **The process documents govern the corpus and are not part of it.** They are
# tracked here and excluded from the ingest, because a gate reading its own
# rulebook as subject matter is a category error - it cost three review rounds
# on PR #570 before it was named. An earlier form of this stanza said they had
# left the repository. They had not, and the sentence framed a live rule as a
# legacy safety net a later act could delete. The rule is current.
#
# **The contents are excluded and not the directory, because `gates/` and
# `ingest/` are named back in.** gitignore cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so `process/` followed by `!process/gates/` reads as a
# rule and does nothing: the walk never descends far enough to see the negation.
# Excluding `process/*` prunes the four documents sitting directly under it while
# leaving the directory walkable, and `*` does not match `/`, so nothing deeper is
# caught by that line.
#
# **The two named back are instruments and not documents**, which is the same
# distinction PR #570 drew: `census.py` is H6, `test_census.py` holds it to its
# baseline, and `chunk_plan.py --check` is a gate CLAUDE.md names. A gate that is
# not in the graph cannot be found by a query about the gates, and these three
# were out of it for as long as this entry read `process/`.
process/*
!process/gates/
!process/ingest/

# **Working registers, sketches and dated readings stay in the repository and
# out of the graph.** They decide nothing and declare nothing: every one of the
# corpus's 491 nodes and 665 edges is declared under `docs/crates/`, measured
# 2026-09-14, and these files contribute none. Ingesting them puts scaffolding
# beside the set it scaffolded, and puts an audit inside the graph it audits.
docs/project/

# **Test fixtures are data, not corpus.** Five raw float tables under
# `crates/weaver-spu/tests/fixtures/gptoss/` were ingested as documents - twenty
# three lines of numbers carrying a vector each - while the `.ndjson` fixtures
# beside them under `weaver-analysis` were not, purely by extension. A class rule
# rather than an instance, in the shape the archive stanza already argues for.
**/tests/fixtures/
